ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE ACT ON THE MANAGEMENT OF ZOOS AND AQUARIUMS
[Annex 1] <Amended on January 2, 2026>

Requirements for permission for zoos and aquariums (related to Article 9(1))

1. Habitat standards by species of resident animal and specialist personnel standards by size of zoo or aquarium

  a. Zoos

    1) Habitat standards by species of resident animal

      a) General standards
        (1) Provide animal housing facilities and areas appropriate to the ecological characteristics of resident animals.
        (2) Provide facilities or equipment to supply food of appropriate quantities and forms and clean drinking water safely, taking account of the feeding characteristics of resident animals.
        (3) Install fences, moats, partition walls, wire mesh, or similar barriers at housing and exhibition facilities to prevent animals from escaping and ensure visitor safety.
        (4) Install items or facilities for behavioral enrichment.
        (5) Eliminate or minimize factors through which visitors may stimulate resident animals abnormally.
        (6) Provide quarantine or disease prevention facilities and treatment or isolation facilities for disease management of resident animals.

      b) Standards by species
        (1) Mammals
          (a) Provide natural light regularly for diurnal mammals.
          (b) Install hiding places and sleeping places, taking account of the characteristics of resident animals.
          (c) Where breeding is required, provide space for giving birth and caring for young.
        (2) Birds
          (a) Provide natural light regularly. However, artificial lighting may be used where it is difficult to provide natural light.
          (b) Provide resting places, such as perches, taking account of the characteristics of resident animals.
          (c) Where breeding is required, provide nesting sites for egg laying.
        (3) Reptiles and amphibians
          (a) Provide natural light regularly. However, artificial lighting may be used where it is difficult to provide natural light.
          (b) For aquatic animals, provide an aquatic environment suited to their characteristics, for example by supplying flowing water or changing the water regularly.

    2) Specialist personnel standards by size

      a) Where the total number of resident animal species is at least 70
        (1) Have at least one veterinarian, including a part-time veterinarian. However, where animals prescribed by Joint Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries (hereinafter referred to as "animals requiring additional specialist personnel") are kept, have at least one full-time veterinarian or at least two part-time veterinarians. "Full-time" includes reduced working hours during childcare under Article 19-2 of the Equal Employment Opportunity and Work-Family Balance Assistance Act; the same applies throughout this Annex.
        (2) Have at least two persons falling under any of the following categories for each taxonomic group of zoo resident animals specified in the Note:
          (a) A person qualified under the National Technical Qualifications Act as a Professional Engineer, Engineer, Industrial Engineer, or Craftsman in livestock; a Professional Engineer in natural environment management; an Engineer in biological classification (animals); or an Engineer or Industrial Engineer in natural ecological restoration.
          (b) A person who has obtained at least an associate degree in a relevant department, such as veterinary medicine, animal science, zoology, animal resources, companion animal studies, biology, ecology or physiology, or forest resources, at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, and thereafter worked in a relevant field for at least one year.
          (c) A person qualified as a veterinary technician under Article 2(3-2) of the Veterinarians Act.
          (d) A person who has performed animal husbandry and management duties for at least two years.

      b) Where the total number of resident animal species is fewer than 70
        (1) Have at least one veterinarian, including a part-time veterinarian. However, where animals requiring additional specialist personnel are kept, have at least one full-time veterinarian or at least two part-time veterinarians.
        (2) Irrespective of the taxonomic groups of zoo resident animals specified in the Note, have persons falling under any of a)(2)(a) through (d) in the following numbers:
          (a) Where animals requiring additional specialist personnel are kept: at least three persons.
          (b) Where animals requiring additional specialist personnel are not kept: at least two persons.

    Note: The taxonomic groups of zoo resident animals are as follows:
      1. Mammals.
      2. Birds.
      3. Reptiles and amphibians.

  b. Aquariums

    1) Habitat standards by species of resident animal

      a) General standards
        (1) Aquarium tanks shall have appropriate dimensions and water depths suited to the characteristics of resident animals.
        (2) Provide facilities or equipment for keeping water and food supplied to resident animals clean and fresh.
        (3) Manage husbandry conditions, including water temperature, air temperature and humidity, lighting, and noise, separately according to the characteristics of resident animals.
        (4) Provide facilities and equipment for measuring the quality of the water in which animals are kept and maintain an appropriate water quality management program.
        (5) Provide a Life Support System (LSS), including filtration and disinfection facilities, to maintain the hygiene of the water in which animals are kept.
        (6) Provide facilities and equipment for a stable supply of water and electricity even in emergencies.
        (7) Provide items or facilities for behavioral enrichment of resident animals.
        (8) Provide quarantine or disease prevention facilities and treatment or isolation facilities for disease management of resident animals.

      b) Standards by species
        (1) For mammals limited to the order Cetacea, provide separately partitioned reserve tanks and treatment space for treatment and separate husbandry.
        (2) For mammals that are pinnipeds (meaning the group of mammals with flippers; hereinafter the same applies), provide land space in addition to tanks so that they can live both in water and on land.
        (3) For reptiles limited to sea turtles, provide isolation tanks for separation and protection in the event of fighting between individuals or disease; where breeding is required, provide sandy egg-laying areas.
        (4) For birds limited to penguins, provide land space for use as hiding places and nests and as space for walking.

    2) Specialist personnel standards by size

      a) Where the tank floor area is less than 10,000 square meters and the total number of resident animal species is fewer than 200
        (1) Have at least one person who is either a veterinarian, including a part-time veterinarian, or an aquatic organism disease inspector.
        (2) Have at least two persons falling under any of the following categories for each taxonomic group of aquarium resident animals specified in Note 1. However, where the total number of resident animal species is fewer than 70, only one person for each taxonomic group may be employed.
          (a) A person qualified as a Professional Engineer or Engineer in aquaculture under the National Technical Qualifications Act.
          (b) A person who has obtained at least an associate degree in a relevant department, such as veterinary medicine, aquatic biomedicine, fisheries science, aquaculture, animal resources, companion animal studies, or biology, at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act.
          (c) A person who has performed duties concerning aquatic organism husbandry and management for at least two years.

      b) Where the tank floor area is at least 10,000 square meters or the total number of resident animal species is at least 200
        (1) Have at least two persons who are veterinarians, including part-time veterinarians, or aquatic organism disease inspectors.
        (2) Have at least three persons falling under any of a)(2)(a) through (c) for each taxonomic group of aquarium resident animals specified in Note 1.

    Notes:
      1. The taxonomic groups of aquarium resident animals are as follows:
        a. Mammals, reptiles, and birds.
        b. Fish, amphibians, invertebrates, and other marine animals.
      2. Where pinnipeds among mammals, sea turtles among reptiles, or penguins among birds are kept, specialist personnel must include at least one veterinarian, including a part-time veterinarian.

2. Disease management plan for resident animals

  a. The disease management plan for resident animals shall include the following matters:
    1) Management of treatment records.
    2) Disease prevention and biosecurity management for resident animals.
    3) Management of medicines for resident animals.
    4) Quarantine procedures and methods relating to the entry and movement in and out of animals.
    5) Management and disposal methods for infectious waste.
    6) Prevention and management of zoonotic infections in resident animals and their managers.
    7) Measures to prevent spread following an outbreak of a disease specified in any subparagraph of Article 17(2) of the Act, and disclosure of the current status of resident animals.
    8) Procedures and methods for necropsy and disposal of animals that have died.
    9) Regular disease examinations by veterinarians or aquatic organism disease inspectors.
  b. Designate a person responsible for implementing the disease management plan and persons responsible for detailed management of its implementation.

3. Safety management plan for a zoo or aquarium

  a. The safety management plan for a zoo or aquarium shall include the following matters:
    1) Safety management of facilities:
      a) Status of installation, maintenance, and management of facilities to prevent resident animals from escaping.
      b) Facilities and management to prevent resident animals from being injured inside their housing facilities.
      c) Measures to ensure animal safety when designing and constructing animal housing facilities, including the use of durable and fire-resistant materials.
      d) Measures for a stable supply of seawater and management of water temperature and quality during emergencies such as power failures, and measures for management of life support systems.
    2) Response when resident animals escape:
      a) Capture plans and methods.
      b) Operation of capture teams.
      c) Management of capture equipment.
      d) Establishment of an emergency contact network.
    3) Management of dangerous animals, such as wild beasts and highly venomous animals.
    4) Safety of managers.
    5) Safety of visitors.
    6) Evacuation of people and resident animals in the event of fire or disaster.
  b. Designate a person responsible for implementing the safety management plan and persons responsible for detailed management of its implementation.

4. Management plan for resident animals during temporary or permanent closure of a zoo or aquarium

  The management plan for resident animals during temporary or permanent closure of a zoo or aquarium shall include the following matters:

  a. Management and maintenance of records of resident animals:
    1) Records of species names and origins.
    2) Records of dates of entry and removal.
    3) Plans for management of the number of individuals and animal identification tags.
  b. Transfer, entry, or removal of resident animals:
    1) Matters agreed upon with institutions to which resident animals will be sent or from which they will be received.
    2) Procedures and plans for transfer of animals upon permanent closure.
    3) Plans for transport procedures and methods for resident animals.
  c. Management of resident animals during temporary closure or after permanent closure:
    1) Managers and work schedules.
    2) Plans for maintaining and managing animal houses.
    3) Plans for managing and supplying food for resident animals.
    4) Plans for seawater supply and water temperature and quality management in aquariums.

5. Plan for education using resident animals

  The plan for education using resident animals shall include the following matters:

  a. Objectives of operating educational programs using resident animals.
  b. Locations for educational programs for each resident animal and management of facilities.
  c. Operation of educational programs for each species and individual resident animal:
    1) Location and time of educational programs.
    2) Specific methods of conducting educational programs.
    3) Where contact with animals is necessary for educational purposes, the parts to be touched and duration of contact.
    4) Where feeding is necessary for educational purposes, the types and quantities of food and the methods of feeding.
  d. Safety of resident animals.
  e. Hygiene and safety of visitors.
  f. Education of animal keepers and support personnel.

6. Plan for promoting the welfare of resident animals

  The plan for promoting the welfare of resident animals shall include the following matters:

  a. Behavioral enrichment, including the provision of trees, rocks, and pools of water and operation of training programs.
  b. Positive reinforcement, including stress reduction and reduction of stereotypic behavior (meaning abnormal, purposeless behavior that is repetitive and persistent).
  c. Education and enhancement of the expertise of aquarium practitioners to promote welfare.
  d. Evaluation of the welfare conditions of resident animals and improvement of their welfare.

