Criteria for imposition of administrative fines (related to Article 26)

1. General criteria

  a. Aggravated criteria based on the number of violations apply where an administrative fine has been imposed for the same violation within the preceding year. The period shall be calculated by reference to the date on which the administrative fine was imposed for the violation and the date on which the same violation committed again after that disposition was detected.
  b. When imposing an aggravated disposition under item a, the applicable level of aggravation shall be the level following that of the disposition imposed before the violation. Where two or more administrative fines were imposed within the period under item a, the higher level shall be used.
  c. The imposing authority may reduce the amount of the administrative fine under the individual criteria in paragraph 2 by up to one half in any of the following cases. However, this does not apply to an offender who is in arrears on administrative fines:
    1) Where the violation is recognized as resulting from minor negligence or error.
    2) Where the offender is recognized as having made efforts to correct or eliminate the unlawful condition.
    3) Where a reduction is otherwise considered necessary in view of the severity of the violation, its motive and consequences, and other relevant circumstances.
  d. The imposing authority may increase the amount of the administrative fine under the individual criteria in paragraph 2 by up to one half in any of the following cases. Even when increased, the amount may not exceed the maximum administrative fine under Article 32(1) of the Act:
    1) Where the nature and severity of the violation are serious and the harm to the management of the zoo or aquarium, the welfare of resident animals, or similar interests is considered substantial.
    2) Where the unlawful condition has continued for at least six months.
    3) Where an increase is otherwise considered necessary in view of the severity of the violation, its motive and consequences, and other relevant circumstances.

